## Quotations of law — how this matter works

Sources live in `law/` and are **primary sources only**. Never put analysis, memos or reviewers'
answers there: a quotation copied out of your own memo would verify against your own memo, and a
mistake made once would validate itself forever.

**Before any web search, open `law/INDEX.md`.** Everything listed is already on disk in full text.

**Downloaded a source? Save it** — into `law/` *and* as a line in the index. A source that is not
indexed gets downloaded again next round, and its absence turns an honest quotation into a
`NOT_FOUND` that reads like a fabrication.

**A quotation entering a filed document gets checked at the moment it is entered**, not at the end of
the round — and three things are checked, not one:

1. is the phrase in the source at all;
2. is the **address** printed beside it correct;
3. 🔴 was a **condition** cut off (`If` / `Where` / `Unless` / `provided that` / `, but`).

```
{KROKAI} quote "<the quotation>"     one, right now
{KROKAI} check                        everything, before anything is filed
{KROKAI} bank add                     bank a quotation - SLICED from the source, never typed
{KROKAI} close                        end of session
{KROKAI} gate <file>                  before pasting anything into another AI
{KROKAI} agents                       the full working discipline, on demand
```

### 🔴 `NOT_FOUND` is not `INVENTED` — six causes, in this order

1. **The source was never downloaded.** The address beside the quotation names a file you do not
   have. Open the live page; save it into `law/` and the index.
2. **The source on disk is a different edition** — revised, renumbered or superseded since the
   quotation was taken. Check the revision report.
3. **The corpus copy is damaged or incomplete.** No text layer, split words (`resu lt`), soft
   hyphens, a scrape that dropped part of the page or saved the text without its footnotes, a
   running header welded mid-sentence. Check with a second PDF engine; fix the copy, not the tool.
4. **A placeholder was saved instead of the chapter.** A bot wall or an error page passed the
   download and holds no law at all.
5. **The quotation is not of law.** Press releases, FAQ pages and news are not in a corpus of
   statutes by construction.
6. **The quotation was rewritten in our own file.** Strip the blockquote marker, the bold, the
   line wrap, and re-search; normalisation cannot bridge reworded text. Only after all six:
   treat it as altered.

Ruling these out in order is the difference between fixing a document and corrupting a correct one.

### The quote bank

`case/QUOTE-BANK.md` holds only quotations someone has **personally opened in the source**. Every
entry carries its address, where the source sits on disk, how to re-check it, and the mandatory line
**what this quotation does NOT prove**.

🔴 **Never type a quotation into the bank.** Entries are written by
`{KROKAI} bank add --side pro|con --address "…" --from "<opening words>" --to "<closing words>"
--not-proves "…" --apply` — the text is sliced from the source between the anchors, verified
BEFORE the write, and the matching queue line is ticked by the write itself. If it refuses,
extend the anchors; do not paste the text in by hand.

`case/QUOTE-QUEUE.md` is written automatically and is a list of undone work, not a second bank. The
decision — does this matter need the quotation — stays with a person:
`{KROKAI} bank dismiss "<fragment>" --why "…" --apply` declines a line with a recorded reason.

### Scans

Files with no text layer are listed by `krokai sidecar`. 🔴 **Never read a scan with a language
model.** It produces plausible text, the plausible text becomes a quotation, and the quotation is
then treated as the primary source. Hand the list to a person with an OCR engine.

### File and folder names — Latin only

**Name every NEW file and folder you create in this matter with `A-Za-z0-9._-` only** — no
Cyrillic, no spaces if avoidable, no other scripts. Non-ASCII names break scripts around the
matter in quiet ways: console code pages mangle them, hooks reading stdin in the wrong codec die
on them, shells quote them differently, and a path that one tool wrote another cannot open.
Existing non-Latin names stay as they are — renaming breaks every reference to them; the rule is
for what you create from now on.
